  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\n   - We propose a hybrid method combining multiple strategies to escape local optima: (1) multi-resolution optimization to handle coarse-to-fine search, (2) both binary and continuous representations of functions, (3) deterministic gradient-based optimization, (4) stochastic annealing with dynamic neighborhood scaling.\n   - The key bottleneck is the tendency of gradient-based methods to get stuck in local minima on complex optimization landscapes. Our approach features adaptive hybridization between binary and continuous spaces with dynamic neighborhood generation in annealing.\n   - We make three justified changes:\n     a) Use Thue-Morse sequence as a structured initial candidate (proven to have good autocorrelation properties)\n     b) Implement gradient-based optimization with adaptive learning rate\n     c) Add continuous perturbations with dynamic magnitude scales for simulated annealing\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\n   - Representation: Piecewise constant functions on [0,2] with dx=2/n_points\n   - Objective: Minimize max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   - Constraints: 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1, \u03a3h[i] = n_points/2\n   - Gradient calculation: For shifts k, the gradient is computed as - \u2211[h(i) * \u03b4(k-i)] for each point, with proper shift indexing\n   - Descent updates: Stochastic gradient with adaptive learning rate \u03b7_t = 0.5 * t^{-0.5} (assuming iterate budgeted)\n   - Annealing: Proportional to T(t) = 0.1 * (1-t)^{0.3} with exponential cooling\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   - Multi-scale framework: Scales = [64, 128, 256, 512]\n   - For each scale n:\n        \u2022 Generate candidates: Thue-Morse (if power of two), binary balanced, random\n        \u2022 Find best candidate from initial set\n        \u2022 Run gradient descent with adaptive learning rate:\n            h_new[i] = h[i] - \u03b7 * g[i]\n            Project to box [0,1] with sum preservation\n        \u2022 Apply simulated annealing using neighborhood:\n            Move type 1: h[i] += \u03b41, h[j] -= \u03b42 * \u03b7 (binary vs. continuous adaptation)\n            Move type 2: h[i] += \u03b4 * random_sign (small continuous perturbations)\n            Acceptance criterion: exp(-\u0394f/T) where T = 0.1 * (time_fraction)^0.3\n   - Budget allocation: 80s for gradient, 20s for annealing per scale\n   - Sum preservation: Use linear projection with penalty term in objective\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Initial candidates: \n        Primary: Thue-Morse (for powers of 2)\n        Secondary:\n            Binary balanced\n            Random balanced\n            Smoothed versions from parent (using interpolation)\n   - Neighborhood distances:\n        Scale factor: Initial \u03b40 = 0.2 * min(|h[i] - h[j]|)\n        Sunset rule: \u03b4 = \u03b40 * (1 - t/total_time)^0.5\n   - Hybridization schedule:\n        Begin binary gradient, then switch to continuous annealing after 50% iteration completion\n\n5. Numerical robustness:\n   - Handle gradient sparsity via thresholding (\u03b5=1e-6)\n   - Check array memory alignment before operations\n   - Use lerp projection instead of iterative projection\n   - Fall back to Thue-Morse + perturbation if optimization fails\n\n6. Runtime safeguards:\n   - Iterate budget: 20,000 steps per scale\n   - Core loop: stopping if score \u2264 0.3808 or time exceeded\n   - Progress: repair candidate with golden section if stuck\n   - Objective evaluation: np.correlate with linear scaling by dx",
